    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Maya religion is to a large extent a complex of ritual practices, sometimes called costumbre: offerings, prayer, purification and sacrifice, with landmarks such as mountains, caves and cenotes as sacred places.</li><li>The 260-day calendar, still used by daykeepers, guides life through the 20 named days and 13 numbers, and the Year Bearers and katuns were worshipped as divine lords.</li><li>Its pre-Hispanic form is known from Maya codices, inscriptions and pottery scenes and from Spanish colonial writers, and today it is the product of centuries of symbiosis with Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A drink offered at a pre-Hispanic New Year ritual was to be made of exactly 415 grains of parched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and on another occasion exactly 49 grains of maize mixed with copal were to be burnt.</p></aside>
    </section>

